  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nA mid-level shortwave (25-30 knots of WSW 700 mb flow) will traverse\nthe base of an upper-level trough Wednesday into Thursday across\nthe Great Lakes as a cold front sweeps east through the area on\nThursday. Showers and thunderstorms will develop ahead of the \nfront Wednesday afternoon and evening, primarily impacting \nportions of Northwest Ohio along the I-75 corridor, and again \nThursday afternoon, generally east of the I-77 corridor. The \ngreatest threat for organized thunderstorms on Wednesday will \nbe west of the area across portions of north-central Indiana, \nthough depending on the eastward extent of the instability \ngradient, a few of our counties along the I-75 corridor across \nNorthwest Ohio could see some strong to severe storms as well, \ngenerally between 6 and 10 PM. Damaging wind gusts up to 60 mph \nappear to be the primary concern, though large hail up to 1 inch\nand a brief tornado also cannot be ruled out. \n\nShowers and thunderstorms should largely diminish overnight as the \nshortwave swings east through the Ohio Valley, though redevelopment \nis possible again Thursday afternoon with the aid of diurnal \nheating, mainly east of the I-77 corridor. The latest guidance \nsuggests a decreasing risk for strong to severe storms Thursday \nafternoon as the greatest coverage of storms continues to trend \nfurther east of the area into Pennsylvania and Southeast Ohio. \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2.. \nAfter a brief stint of drier and seasonable weather, a large \nupper-level ridge will enter the region towards the end of the \nweekend into early next week with increasing temperatures and \nhumidity. Highs will reach above average in the mid to upper 80s\non Sunday, potentially touching 90 in some spots by Monday. In \naddition, we'll also have to continue to monitor the potential \nfor showers and thunderstorms, especially Monday night through \nTuesday as an upper-level trough swings east through the Upper \nGreat Lakes.",
